The aim of this pull request is to give total control over `Authentication` instance to the framework user after successful introspection.

> this solution only works when the default converter is used

This is exactly my intention. Concider following sample (which compiles):
```java
public static class KeycloakClaims extends HashMap<String, Object> implements Serializable, StandardClaimAccessor {
    public KeycloakClaims(Map<String, Object> m) {
        super(m);
    }

    @Override
    public Map<String, Object> getClaims() {
        return this;
    }
    
    public List<String> getRoles() {
        final var realmAccess = (Map<String, Object>) getOrDefault("realm_access", Map.of());
        return (List<String>) realmAccess.getOrDefault("roles", List.of());
    }
}

public static class KeycloakAuthentication extends AbstractAuthenticationToken {
    private final String bearerString;
    
    //This constructor can be used as OpaqueTokenAuthenticationConverter as is
    //Providing a JWT authentication converter out of it is trivial: jwt -> new KeycloakAuthentication(jwt.getTokenValue(), jwt.getClaims())
    public KeycloakAuthentication (String bearerString, Map<String, Object> m) {
        this(bearerString, new KeycloakClaims(m));
    }

    private KeycloakAuthentication (String bearerString, KeycloakClaims claims) {
        super(claims.getRoles().stream().map(SimpleGrantedAuthority::new).toList());
        this.bearerString = bearerString;
        this.setDetails(claims);
    }

    @Override
    public void setDetails(Object details) {
        assert(KeycloakClaims.class.isAssignableFrom(details.getClass()));
        super.setDetails(details);
    }

    @Override
    public KeycloakClaims getDetails() {
        return (KeycloakClaims)super.getDetails();
    }

    @Override
    public KeycloakClaims getCredentials() {
        return this.getDetails();
    }

    @Override
    public KeycloakClaims getPrincipal() {
        return this.getDetails();
    }
    
    public String getBearerString() {
        return this.bearerString;
    }
}
```
Clearly, trying to set details with a random `Object` on such an `AbstractAuthenticationToken` implementation will be a problem. And also, there is no need to do it as constructor (which can be used as `OpaqueTokenAuthenticationConverter`) already set `details` property.

I understand the need to keep exact same behaviour for **default** authentication converter, but I think it would be very tricky (and frustrating) for one explicitly providing an `OpaqueTokenAuthenticationConverter` to have the authentication instance it returned be arbitrarly altered with a value out of his control.

## PS
> it's fairly common for a filter or provider to set the details afterwards

With a notable exception of `OpaqueTokenReactiveAuthenticationManager`. Is it intended or just a bug introduced when duplicating code?

Those are good points, @ch4mpy.

> The aim of this pull request is to give total control over `Authentication` instance to the framework user after successful introspection.

It may be that a converter is not the best solution in that case? Introducing a converter adds a configuration hook that gives flexibility to the user. If total control is the goal, there are other ways to accomplish that. For example, we could build a delegating `AuthenticationProvider`:

```java
public class MyOpaqueTokenAuthenticationProvider implements AuthenticationProvider {

	private final AuthenticationProvider delegate;

	public MyOpaqueTokenAuthenticationProvider(OpaqueTokenIntrospector introspector) {
		this.delegate = new OpaqueTokenAuthenticationProvider(introspector);
	}

	@Override
	public boolean supports(Class<?> authentication) {
		return this.delegate.supports(authentication);
	}

	@Override
	public Authentication authenticate(Authentication authentication) throws AuthenticationException {
		Authentication result = this.delegate.authenticate(authentication);
		// ...
	}

}
```

This gives full control at the expense of constructing a new authentication based on the returned one. Of course, you can always choose to fully implement the `AuthenticationProvider` yourself if needed.

Having said that, it sounds like my suggestion conflicts with your intended use case for introducing the converter? If that's the case, perhaps your authentication instance shouldn't extend from `AbstractAuthenticationToken`? Or it could simply store the claims in another field, which seems to make sense here. The claims in your example seem more specific than `Object details` from `AbstractAuthenticationToken`.

> As far I can read from existing code, `details` are set for servlets only. Is it intended or just a bug introduced when duplicating code in `OpaqueTokenReactiveAuthenticationManager`?

I'd have to dig into it to say for 100% certain, but it could be that the use of `details` addresses a servlet-specific need.

